  "result": "I opened the issue on `acme/textstats`, the repo `gh` resolved to for this checkout: https://github.com/acme/textstats/issues/13\n\nI confirmed the bug before filing it. `word_count` in `textstats/__init__.py:4` uses `len(text.split(\" \"))`, and `\"\".split(\" \")` returns `[\"\"]`, so an empty string counts as 1 word.\n\nThe same code also counts extra words in other cases, and the issue covers these too:\n- **Spaces only:** `\"   \"` returns 4.\n- **Double spaces:** `\"a  b\"` returns 3.\n\nThe issue suggests switching to `text.split()` with no arguments, which `average_word_length` already uses, and adding an empty-string test. I didn't change any code.",
